  "statements": {
    "linear-momentum": "Linear momentum is the product of a system’s mass and its velocity, and it is the quantity that says how hard a moving object is to stop. A large, fast-moving object has greater momentum than a smaller, slower one.",
    "calculate-momentum": "Momentum is a vector with the same direction as the velocity, and its SI unit is the kilogram meter per second. Multiplying a mass by a velocity, keeping the direction and the unit, gives the momentum from which a collision problem starts.",
    "constant-mass-second-law": "When the mass of the system does not change, the change in momentum is the mass times the change in velocity, and dividing by the time gives the acceleration. The familiar form of the second law, $F_{\\text{net}} = ma$, is therefore the special case of the momentum form for a constant mass.",
    "change-in-momentum": "The change in momentum is the difference between the final and the initial momentum, and for a constant mass it is the mass times the change in velocity. It is the quantity that a force acting for a time produces.",
    "average-force-from-impulse": "The average force of a collision is found from the mass and the velocities before and after it: form the change in momentum, keeping its direction, and divide by the time of contact.",
    "rocket-acceleration": "Equating the change in momentum of the whole system over a short time to the impulse of gravity gives a good approximation for the acceleration of what is left of the rocket, $a = (v_{\\text{e}}/m)(\\Delta m/\\Delta t) - g$. It describes a system whose mass changes as it moves."
  },
